The paper studies an Einstein–Yang–Mills model in higher dimensions that includes higher-derivative Yang–Mills terms, focusing on spontaneous compactification where the extra dimensions form a six-sphere \(S^6\).

Its main point is that a stable compactification of the form

\[
\text{4D spacetime} \times S^6
\]

can arise dynamically when the Yang–Mills fields on \(S^6\) take a special Cremmer–Scherk configuration. The higher-derivative Yang–Mills term is crucial because it allows the gauge-field energy to satisfy a Bogomol’nyi-type bound, stabilizing the radius of the compact sphere.

The abstract emphasizes that:

- the theory extends ordinary Einstein–Yang–Mills theory by adding higher-order Yang–Mills curvature terms;
- the six-dimensional internal space is compactified as \(S^6\);
- a particular gauge-field configuration on \(S^6\) supports this compactification;
- the compactification is stable due to a self-duality/Bogomol’nyi structure;
- the resulting four-dimensional effective theory can have a small or vanishing cosmological constant depending on parameter choices.

In short, the paper shows that higher-derivative Yang–Mills interactions can naturally stabilize extra dimensions through a nontrivial gauge-field background on \(S^6\).
